WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.378 --> 00:00:08.278
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:08.278 --> 00:00:10.178
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:10.178 --> 00:00:13.108
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:13.118 --> 00:00:14.898
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.898 --> 00:00:17.868
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.878 --> 00:00:19.888
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.888 --> 00:00:21.708
time as improving your English.

00:00:22.108 --> 00:00:25.172
I'm Alastair Budge, and today it's the

00:00:25.172 --> 00:00:28.532
start of another three-part mini-series,

00:00:28.912 --> 00:00:32.532
this time on The Dark Side of Psychology,

00:00:32.822 --> 00:00:35.302
where we'll look at three of the most

00:00:35.302 --> 00:00:38.302
famous and uncomfortable experiments in

00:00:38.302 --> 00:00:40.992
the history of the science of the mind.

00:00:41.372 --> 00:00:43.422
In today's episode, we'll look at

00:00:43.432 --> 00:00:45.852
the Milgram experiment, which sought

00:00:45.962 --> 00:00:48.482
to find out what ordinary people

00:00:48.482 --> 00:00:51.372
do when an authority figure tells

00:00:51.372 --> 00:00:53.002
them to do something terrible.

00:00:53.382 --> 00:00:56.152
In part two, we'll turn to the work of

00:00:56.162 --> 00:00:59.362
Harry Harlow, the American psychologist

00:00:59.582 --> 00:01:01.922
whose disturbing experiments with baby

00:01:01.922 --> 00:01:05.262
monkeys changed our understanding of love.

00:01:05.702 --> 00:01:08.162
And in part three, we'll travel to

00:01:08.162 --> 00:01:11.592
the psychiatric hospitals of 1970s

00:01:11.592 --> 00:01:14.092
America, where eight perfectly

00:01:14.092 --> 00:01:16.382
sane volunteers checked themselves

00:01:16.382 --> 00:01:18.322
in to see what would happen.

00:01:18.772 --> 00:01:21.122
OK then, let's not waste a

00:01:21.132 --> 00:01:23.812
minute and get right into it.

00:01:24.942 --> 00:01:27.629
The year is 1961.

00:01:28.322 --> 00:01:30.992
It is April, and in Jerusalem,

00:01:31.342 --> 00:01:33.832
a trial has just begun.

00:01:34.752 --> 00:01:38.352
The man on the stand is Adolf Eichmann.

00:01:39.242 --> 00:01:41.352
Eichmann had been one of the

00:01:41.372 --> 00:01:44.582
central architects of the Holocaust,

00:01:44.922 --> 00:01:47.132
the Nazi programme to murder

00:01:47.412 --> 00:01:49.602
every Jewish person in Europe.

00:01:50.792 --> 00:01:53.572
In his role as a senior officer

00:01:53.762 --> 00:01:56.672
of the SS, he had organised

00:01:56.932 --> 00:01:59.802
the logistics of mass murder.

00:02:00.672 --> 00:02:02.812
He drew up the timetables for

00:02:02.862 --> 00:02:04.972
the trains that carried millions

00:02:04.972 --> 00:02:07.072
of people to the death camps.

00:02:07.342 --> 00:02:09.102
He coordinated the paperwork

00:02:09.272 --> 00:02:11.142
between police forces and

00:02:11.152 --> 00:02:13.192
administrative departments across

00:02:13.222 --> 00:02:15.332
a dozen occupied countries.

00:02:16.082 --> 00:02:19.532
He may never have personally killed

00:02:19.552 --> 00:02:23.422
anyone, but he was very far from innocent.

00:02:24.542 --> 00:02:27.472
If the Holocaust was an industrial

00:02:27.512 --> 00:02:30.192
process, Eichmann was one

00:02:30.222 --> 00:02:32.502
of its principal engineers.

00:02:33.322 --> 00:02:36.692
He had escaped to Argentina after the war

00:02:36.962 --> 00:02:39.882
and had been living under a false name.

00:02:40.682 --> 00:02:43.452
The Israeli secret service, Mossad,

00:02:43.702 --> 00:02:46.952
tracked him down in 1960 and brought

00:02:46.952 --> 00:02:49.562
him to Israel to stand trial.

00:02:50.832 --> 00:02:53.622
The trial was broadcast on television

00:02:53.962 --> 00:02:56.202
and followed around the world.

00:02:57.262 --> 00:02:58.562
For the first time,

00:02:58.992 --> 00:03:01.302
people saw Adolf Eichmann.

00:03:02.392 --> 00:03:04.912
And many were surprised.

00:03:05.882 --> 00:03:08.022
Given what he had done, many

00:03:08.022 --> 00:03:11.052
people had expected to see a

00:03:11.052 --> 00:03:14.112
monster, someone who clearly looked

00:03:14.132 --> 00:03:17.202
evil, threatening and menacing.

00:03:18.572 --> 00:03:22.662
Instead, what they saw was a thin,

00:03:22.992 --> 00:03:26.472
bespectacled, rather ordinary-looking man.

00:03:26.972 --> 00:03:28.942
A man who wouldn't have looked

00:03:28.992 --> 00:03:31.172
out of place teaching at a school,

00:03:31.222 --> 00:03:33.322
knocking on your door to sell you life

00:03:33.322 --> 00:03:36.022
insurance, or working in a bookshop.

00:03:36.772 --> 00:03:38.742
In other words, a perfectly

00:03:39.082 --> 00:03:40.532
normal looking man.

00:03:42.232 --> 00:03:45.682
During the trial, as he sat in a glass

00:03:45.682 --> 00:03:48.492
box, he kept saying the same thing:

00:03:49.211 --> 00:03:50.872
he'd just been following orders.

00:03:51.722 --> 00:03:54.122
He presented himself not as an

00:03:54.352 --> 00:03:57.712
ideologue or fanatic, but as a

00:03:57.722 --> 00:04:00.572
functionary: a man who had followed

00:04:00.602 --> 00:04:03.502
orders inside a vast bureaucracy.

00:04:04.462 --> 00:04:07.212
He held no personal animosity

00:04:07.432 --> 00:04:09.592
towards Jewish people, he said.

00:04:10.202 --> 00:04:11.832
He had done what he was told,

00:04:12.042 --> 00:04:14.202
within a system, and that

00:04:14.502 --> 00:04:16.462
was what the system required.

00:04:17.402 --> 00:04:20.352
Now, you might believe him, you might not.

00:04:20.802 --> 00:04:22.782
The court did not, and he

00:04:22.782 --> 00:04:24.392
was sentenced to death.

00:04:25.782 --> 00:04:28.222
One person watching closely

00:04:28.622 --> 00:04:30.802
was a German-American political

00:04:30.802 --> 00:04:33.652
philosopher named Hannah Arendt.

00:04:34.642 --> 00:04:37.392
She was covering the trial for the

00:04:37.402 --> 00:04:40.682
New Yorker magazine, and she coined

00:04:40.692 --> 00:04:42.642
a phrase that has been debated

00:04:42.682 --> 00:04:45.962
ever since: the "banality of evil."

00:04:47.492 --> 00:04:50.252
Her argument was that Eichmann was not

00:04:50.292 --> 00:04:52.782
a monster in the traditional sense.

00:04:53.482 --> 00:04:55.992
He was an ordinary bureaucrat who

00:04:55.992 --> 00:04:58.202
had given up moral responsibility

00:04:58.562 --> 00:05:00.712
and simply done his job.

00:05:01.802 --> 00:05:04.632
And that, she argued, was in

00:05:04.632 --> 00:05:07.032
some ways more frightening

00:05:07.312 --> 00:05:10.172
than the idea of pure evil.

00:05:11.252 --> 00:05:13.522
Now, another person watching

00:05:13.522 --> 00:05:15.972
the Eichmann trial closely was a

00:05:15.982 --> 00:05:18.892
young social psychologist at Yale

00:05:18.902 --> 00:05:21.832
University named Stanley Milgram.

00:05:23.312 --> 00:05:25.752
He had been contemplating the idea of

00:05:26.082 --> 00:05:28.952
obedience, and specifically, the German

00:05:28.952 --> 00:05:31.682
people's compliance with the Nazi regime.

00:05:32.902 --> 00:05:35.862
He had come to believe that, under

00:05:35.862 --> 00:05:38.842
the right conditions, ordinary people

00:05:39.382 --> 00:05:42.442
anywhere might be capable of the same

00:05:42.452 --> 00:05:44.872
thing; there was nothing uniquely

00:05:44.982 --> 00:05:47.052
German about what had happened.

00:05:48.312 --> 00:05:52.462
He wondered, could ordinary Americans be

00:05:52.462 --> 00:05:56.312
made to hurt an innocent stranger simply

00:05:56.392 --> 00:05:59.082
because an authority figure told them to?

00:06:00.212 --> 00:06:03.952
So, he designed an experiment to find out.

00:06:05.522 --> 00:06:07.912
Milgram recruited volunteers

00:06:08.142 --> 00:06:09.892
through newspaper advertisements

00:06:10.132 --> 00:06:12.232
for what he described as "a

00:06:12.262 --> 00:06:14.892
study of memory and learning."

00:06:15.692 --> 00:06:18.052
It was a paid experiment, with

00:06:18.052 --> 00:06:20.302
participants getting four dollars

00:06:20.352 --> 00:06:22.782
and fifty cents, which is around

00:06:22.792 --> 00:06:24.652
fifty dollars in today's money.

00:06:25.592 --> 00:06:27.792
Importantly, they would be paid

00:06:27.892 --> 00:06:31.272
regardless; the pay wasn't contingent

00:06:31.492 --> 00:06:32.682
on whether they did what they

00:06:32.682 --> 00:06:34.501
were asked to in the experiment.

00:06:36.042 --> 00:06:39.322
The advert attracted a broad cross-section

00:06:39.332 --> 00:06:42.832
of people: teachers, engineers, postal

00:06:42.832 --> 00:06:45.402
workers, salesmen, people in their

00:06:45.422 --> 00:06:47.882
twenties and their fifties, people

00:06:47.882 --> 00:06:50.142
of different educational backgrounds.

00:06:50.742 --> 00:06:52.842
In other words, it was a decent-enough

00:06:52.902 --> 00:06:55.001
cross-section of society.

00:06:56.721 --> 00:06:59.382
When the volunteers arrived at the

00:06:59.392 --> 00:07:02.172
psychology department at Yale, they

00:07:02.172 --> 00:07:04.985
were met by a man in a grey laboratory

00:07:04.985 --> 00:07:07.222
coat who explained the study.

00:07:08.132 --> 00:07:09.332
There would be three

00:07:09.372 --> 00:07:10.752
people in the experiment.

00:07:11.302 --> 00:07:13.752
The "experimenter", the

00:07:13.772 --> 00:07:16.202
"teacher", and the "learner".

00:07:17.612 --> 00:07:19.702
The "experimenter" was the

00:07:19.712 --> 00:07:22.102
leader of the session, and would

00:07:22.102 --> 00:07:24.482
explain how the study would work.

00:07:25.032 --> 00:07:27.692
He was the figure of authority.

00:07:29.032 --> 00:07:32.002
The two volunteers would be given slips

00:07:32.032 --> 00:07:34.872
of paper to decide which one was the

00:07:34.872 --> 00:07:37.802
"teacher" and which one was the "learner".

00:07:38.982 --> 00:07:41.462
The thing was, only one of the

00:07:41.462 --> 00:07:43.982
volunteers was actually a volunteer.

00:07:44.802 --> 00:07:46.802
The other one was part of the

00:07:46.802 --> 00:07:49.432
experiment team, and was only

00:07:49.492 --> 00:07:51.782
pretending to be a volunteer.

00:07:53.072 --> 00:07:55.422
The reason for this was to make

00:07:55.432 --> 00:07:57.492
the volunteer think that they would

00:07:57.492 --> 00:07:59.792
be randomly assigned as either the

00:07:59.792 --> 00:08:02.732
teacher or the learner, but in fact

00:08:03.302 --> 00:08:06.632
the real volunteer would always be

00:08:06.642 --> 00:08:09.082
the teacher, and the fake volunteer

00:08:09.292 --> 00:08:11.262
would always be the learner.

00:08:12.531 --> 00:08:13.841
They were told it would

00:08:13.851 --> 00:08:15.581
be a memory experiment.

00:08:16.251 --> 00:08:19.181
The teacher would be required to teach

00:08:19.241 --> 00:08:22.091
lists of word pairs to the learner.

00:08:22.941 --> 00:08:26.291
If the learner made a mistake, the teacher

00:08:26.491 --> 00:08:29.061
would administer an electric shock.

00:08:30.071 --> 00:08:32.701
They would start relatively small, at

00:08:32.761 --> 00:08:36.291
15 volts, but every time the learner

00:08:36.291 --> 00:08:39.011
made a mistake, the voltage would

00:08:39.031 --> 00:08:44.761
increase, up to a maximum of 450 volts.

00:08:46.201 --> 00:08:48.321
The learner would be strapped

00:08:48.481 --> 00:08:50.831
into a chair with electrical

00:08:50.831 --> 00:08:53.021
wiring so they couldn't escape.

00:08:54.101 --> 00:08:56.321
And before the experiment started,

00:08:56.811 --> 00:08:59.691
the teacher was given a small electric

00:08:59.691 --> 00:09:02.711
shock at the lowest level, so they

00:09:02.711 --> 00:09:04.991
could feel what the learner would feel.

00:09:05.941 --> 00:09:07.811
The shocks, the experimenter

00:09:07.811 --> 00:09:10.161
explained, could be painful, but

00:09:10.431 --> 00:09:12.771
they would cause no permanent damage.

00:09:14.450 --> 00:09:17.250
Now, the setting mattered

00:09:17.311 --> 00:09:19.791
enormously to Milgram's design.

00:09:20.461 --> 00:09:22.731
This was Yale University,

00:09:22.981 --> 00:09:24.501
one of the most prestigious

00:09:24.541 --> 00:09:26.771
institutions in the United States.

00:09:27.131 --> 00:09:28.811
It stood, in the public

00:09:28.811 --> 00:09:31.451
mind, for seriousness, for

00:09:31.831 --> 00:09:34.371
expertise, for trustworthiness.

00:09:35.001 --> 00:09:37.661
The man in the grey coat spoke

00:09:37.731 --> 00:09:40.171
calmly and authoritatively.

00:09:40.651 --> 00:09:42.061
The equipment looked

00:09:42.081 --> 00:09:43.981
professional and expensive.

00:09:44.541 --> 00:09:46.141
Everything about the physical

00:09:46.161 --> 00:09:49.181
environment communicated one message:

00:09:49.751 --> 00:09:52.601
this is a legitimate scientific

00:09:52.631 --> 00:09:55.621
enterprise conducted by experts who

00:09:55.621 --> 00:09:58.131
know exactly what they are doing.

00:09:59.091 --> 00:09:59.841
Trust us.

00:10:00.311 --> 00:10:02.061
Follow our instructions.

00:10:03.131 --> 00:10:05.331
The shock generator the teacher

00:10:05.331 --> 00:10:07.491
was shown looked convincing.

00:10:08.061 --> 00:10:10.861
It had thirty switches, each labelled

00:10:10.881 --> 00:10:14.051
with a voltage: 15 volts at the lower

00:10:14.051 --> 00:10:18.021
end, up to 450 volts at the higher end.

00:10:18.951 --> 00:10:21.821
The labels above the switches ranged

00:10:21.831 --> 00:10:24.500
from "Slight Shock" through to "Moderate

00:10:24.531 --> 00:10:26.791
Shock," "Strong Shock," "Very Strong

00:10:26.791 --> 00:10:29.631
Shock," "Intense Shock," and "Extreme

00:10:29.671 --> 00:10:33.221
Intensity Shock," to a final cluster of

00:10:33.301 --> 00:10:38.331
three switches simply labelled "XXX."

00:10:39.461 --> 00:10:41.671
The learner was then taken to an

00:10:41.681 --> 00:10:44.481
adjacent room and, as the teacher

00:10:44.481 --> 00:10:47.291
watched, strapped into a chair.

00:10:48.161 --> 00:10:50.981
Electrodes were attached to his wrist.

00:10:51.591 --> 00:10:53.271
The door was closed.

00:10:54.301 --> 00:10:57.231
Now, to remind you, at this point,

00:10:57.491 --> 00:11:00.041
the teacher believed that they and

00:11:00.051 --> 00:11:02.751
the learner were both volunteers in a

00:11:02.761 --> 00:11:06.041
real experiment about memory, and they

00:11:06.041 --> 00:11:08.151
had been assigned the roles randomly.

00:11:08.941 --> 00:11:11.641
This was, as you heard, not the

00:11:11.641 --> 00:11:14.221
case; the learner was also an

00:11:14.281 --> 00:11:17.111
actor, a member of Milgram's team.

00:11:17.921 --> 00:11:21.231
And there were no actual electric shocks.

00:11:21.531 --> 00:11:23.661
The electrodes were props.

00:11:23.881 --> 00:11:25.511
The shock generator was

00:11:25.511 --> 00:11:26.731
not connected to anything.

00:11:27.571 --> 00:11:30.721
The real experiment was not about memory.

00:11:31.121 --> 00:11:33.341
It was about the teacher, seeing

00:11:33.361 --> 00:11:35.921
how far this person would go.

00:11:37.137 --> 00:11:38.607
The experiment began.

00:11:39.517 --> 00:11:42.417
The learner attempted to answer questions.

00:11:43.087 --> 00:11:45.537
When he got one wrong, and he was

00:11:45.537 --> 00:11:48.547
scripted to get many wrong, the teacher

00:11:48.687 --> 00:11:51.627
was instructed to administer a shock,

00:11:51.927 --> 00:11:54.447
increasing the voltage with each error.

00:11:55.487 --> 00:11:58.717
At 75 volts, the learner grunted.

00:11:59.127 --> 00:12:02.247
At 120 volts, he called out that

00:12:02.247 --> 00:12:04.257
the shocks were becoming painful.

00:12:04.657 --> 00:12:07.817
At 150 volts, he demanded to be

00:12:07.817 --> 00:12:09.077
released from the experiment.

00:12:09.757 --> 00:12:13.267
At 165 volts, he cried out loudly.

00:12:13.667 --> 00:12:16.887
At 180 volts, he shouted that he

00:12:16.887 --> 00:12:18.447
could no longer stand the pain.

00:12:18.847 --> 00:12:22.497
At 300 volts, he refused to answer any

00:12:22.497 --> 00:12:25.497
more questions and begged to be let out.

00:12:25.947 --> 00:12:30.367
At 315 volts, he screamed violently.

00:12:31.097 --> 00:12:35.027
After 330 volts, there was silence.

00:12:35.407 --> 00:12:36.967
Just silence.

00:12:37.317 --> 00:12:39.757
No responses, no movement.

00:12:41.287 --> 00:12:43.617
Now, many of the teachers

00:12:43.667 --> 00:12:45.907
were clearly troubled by this.

00:12:45.997 --> 00:12:49.687
They sweated, trembled, and begged

00:12:49.857 --> 00:12:52.047
the experimenter to let them stop.

00:12:53.427 --> 00:12:55.267
The experimenter, of course,

00:12:55.307 --> 00:12:56.687
was prepared for this.

00:12:57.237 --> 00:12:59.457
Milgram had instructed them

00:12:59.757 --> 00:13:02.077
to give specific responses.

00:13:03.157 --> 00:13:05.837
If the teacher asked to stop, the

00:13:05.917 --> 00:13:08.717
experimenter was to tell them, calmly,

00:13:09.357 --> 00:13:12.017
"Please continue" or "Please go on."

00:13:13.407 --> 00:13:16.307
If they still refused, the experimenter

00:13:16.477 --> 00:13:18.727
told them that, "The experiment

00:13:18.847 --> 00:13:20.507
requires that you continue."

00:13:21.207 --> 00:13:23.367
If they continued to protest,

00:13:23.777 --> 00:13:25.997
they were told "It is absolutely

00:13:25.997 --> 00:13:27.447
essential that you continue."

00:13:28.387 --> 00:13:30.087
And if they refused again,

00:13:30.517 --> 00:13:32.377
they were told, "You have no

00:13:32.377 --> 00:13:34.447
other choice; you must go on."

00:13:35.777 --> 00:13:38.467
The experimenter was always calm and

00:13:38.487 --> 00:13:40.867
collected; he did not raise his voice

00:13:41.137 --> 00:13:43.717
or use any kind of threatening language.

00:13:44.207 --> 00:13:48.887
Clean, clinical, calm and unemotional.

00:13:50.487 --> 00:13:53.487
And as a reminder, the participants,

00:13:53.497 --> 00:13:56.507
the teachers, had been clearly told

00:13:56.707 --> 00:13:58.917
that they could leave at any time.

00:13:59.397 --> 00:14:00.637
Nobody was stopping them.

00:14:01.137 --> 00:14:02.037
They would be paid

00:14:02.297 --> 00:14:04.277
regardless of what happened.

00:14:04.667 --> 00:14:06.887
There was no incentive for them to

00:14:06.887 --> 00:14:09.037
continue, other than because they were

00:14:09.077 --> 00:14:11.947
told to by the man in the grey coat.

00:14:13.257 --> 00:14:15.197
So, what happened?

00:14:15.897 --> 00:14:18.757
Well, before conducting the experiment,

00:14:19.097 --> 00:14:22.027
Milgram had asked his colleagues to

00:14:22.027 --> 00:14:23.867
predict what they thought would happen.

00:14:25.117 --> 00:14:27.577
Most predicted that very few people,

00:14:27.757 --> 00:14:30.477
perhaps one or two per cent, would go

00:14:30.537 --> 00:14:32.577
all the way to the maximum voltage.

00:14:33.317 --> 00:14:36.077
After all, these were random people,

00:14:36.417 --> 00:14:38.717
upstanding citizens, being asked,

00:14:38.847 --> 00:14:41.407
essentially, to torture an innocent

00:14:41.407 --> 00:14:44.067
stranger, someone they believed had

00:14:44.127 --> 00:14:46.657
also volunteered for the experiment.

00:14:48.307 --> 00:14:50.407
Milgram's own estimate was that

00:14:50.407 --> 00:14:53.067
perhaps three per cent would reach

00:14:53.067 --> 00:14:55.477
the highest voltage, and those would

00:14:55.487 --> 00:14:58.197
be, he assumed, individuals with

00:14:58.197 --> 00:15:01.017
some psychological abnormality.

00:15:02.487 --> 00:15:04.677
What actually happened surpassed

00:15:04.737 --> 00:15:06.367
all their expectations.

00:15:07.164 --> 00:15:10.214
Every single participant went at least

00:15:10.244 --> 00:15:13.854
as far as 300 volts, which, let's

00:15:13.854 --> 00:15:15.944
remember, was after the learner had

00:15:15.974 --> 00:15:19.314
screamed in agony and refused to answer.

00:15:20.204 --> 00:15:23.154
And sixty-five per cent of the

00:15:23.154 --> 00:15:25.684
participants administered the full

00:15:25.924 --> 00:15:30.054
450-volt shock, the maximum voltage.

00:15:31.411 --> 00:15:33.781
Some participants said things like,

00:15:34.091 --> 00:15:37.291
“I can’t do this,” paused, looked

00:15:37.301 --> 00:15:39.211
back at the man in the grey coat,

00:15:39.531 --> 00:15:41.771
and then reached for the next switch.

00:15:42.921 --> 00:15:44.711
Milgram was shaken.

00:15:45.111 --> 00:15:47.241
He repeated the experiment multiple

00:15:47.241 --> 00:15:49.231
times, varying the conditions.

00:15:49.781 --> 00:15:52.061
He tried it with women as the teachers.

00:15:52.581 --> 00:15:53.531
Similar results.

00:15:53.851 --> 00:15:55.751
He moved the location from the

00:15:55.781 --> 00:15:58.461
prestigious Yale campus to a run-down

00:15:58.491 --> 00:16:00.621
office building in town, to check

00:16:00.831 --> 00:16:02.681
whether the authority of a famous

00:16:02.681 --> 00:16:04.591
university was doing some of the work.

00:16:05.321 --> 00:16:06.721
The numbers dropped

00:16:06.831 --> 00:16:09.001
slightly, but not by much.

00:16:09.861 --> 00:16:11.571
He tried versions in which the

00:16:11.571 --> 00:16:13.281
learner was in the same room as

00:16:13.281 --> 00:16:15.341
the teacher, so the teacher could

00:16:15.341 --> 00:16:17.481
see them flinching and crying.

00:16:18.361 --> 00:16:20.891
The rates fell, but still

00:16:20.901 --> 00:16:22.201
many people complied.

00:16:23.011 --> 00:16:24.681
He tried versions in which the

00:16:24.681 --> 00:16:26.821
teacher had to physically hold the

00:16:26.821 --> 00:16:29.031
learner's hand onto a shock plate.

00:16:29.891 --> 00:16:32.801
Even then, with the learner visible,

00:16:33.171 --> 00:16:36.451
crying, and begging for it to stop, thirty

00:16:36.451 --> 00:16:39.431
per cent administered the maximum voltage.

00:16:40.557 --> 00:16:43.287
Milgram also tried a version in which the

00:16:43.347 --> 00:16:45.597
experimenter was not in the room at all.

00:16:46.247 --> 00:16:47.517
The instructions were

00:16:47.517 --> 00:16:49.347
given by telephone instead.

00:16:50.237 --> 00:16:52.537
Compliance dropped significantly.

00:16:53.297 --> 00:16:56.627
More people refused, and some of those who

00:16:56.787 --> 00:16:59.957
appeared to comply secretly administered

00:17:00.007 --> 00:17:02.277
lower shocks than the ones they reported.

00:17:03.077 --> 00:17:05.237
The physical presence of the

00:17:05.267 --> 00:17:06.797
authority figure, it turned

00:17:06.797 --> 00:17:08.797
out, was an important factor.

00:17:10.007 --> 00:17:12.467
And he tried a version in which the

00:17:12.467 --> 00:17:14.637
teacher was paired with two other

00:17:14.747 --> 00:17:16.727
"teachers", "teachers" who were

00:17:16.767 --> 00:17:18.727
presented as fellow volunteers,

00:17:19.087 --> 00:17:21.267
but were in fact actors who were

00:17:21.277 --> 00:17:24.357
instructed to refuse at critical points.

00:17:25.107 --> 00:17:28.407
And here, compliance dropped even further.

00:17:29.017 --> 00:17:31.067
It seemed that simply seeing

00:17:31.067 --> 00:17:33.297
someone else refuse planted the

00:17:33.297 --> 00:17:35.687
seed in the participant's head that

00:17:35.697 --> 00:17:37.887
they too could do the same thing.

00:17:39.347 --> 00:17:42.117
Now, Milgram published his findings

00:17:42.317 --> 00:17:45.457
in 1963, and later in a book,

00:17:45.747 --> 00:17:48.581
Obedience to Authority, in 1974.

00:17:49.881 --> 00:17:53.221
In it, he suggested that this experiment

00:17:53.481 --> 00:17:55.961
showed that the capacity for harmful

00:17:55.981 --> 00:17:58.901
obedience to authority is not a special

00:17:58.901 --> 00:18:02.091
property of Germans in the 1930s and 40s.

00:18:02.481 --> 00:18:05.581
It is not unique to people with unusual

00:18:05.601 --> 00:18:07.981
psychological profiles, or to people

00:18:08.111 --> 00:18:10.941
raised in particular ideological systems.

00:18:11.591 --> 00:18:14.291
It appears to be broadly distributed

00:18:14.601 --> 00:18:16.811
across ordinary human beings,

00:18:17.131 --> 00:18:18.831
regardless of nationality,

00:18:19.291 --> 00:18:21.791
education, or personal decency.

00:18:22.751 --> 00:18:24.951
The people who administered those

00:18:24.951 --> 00:18:27.841
shocks were ordinary men and women.

00:18:28.351 --> 00:18:29.911
Many of them were deeply

00:18:29.911 --> 00:18:31.941
distressed by what they were doing.

00:18:32.861 --> 00:18:34.531
And yet they did it.

00:18:35.571 --> 00:18:36.021
Why?

00:18:36.631 --> 00:18:39.301
Well, there are a few theories about

00:18:39.301 --> 00:18:41.841
which factors contributed to this.

00:18:42.771 --> 00:18:44.351
The first is something we've

00:18:44.411 --> 00:18:46.581
touched on already: the structure.

00:18:46.981 --> 00:18:49.081
There was the setting: Yale

00:18:49.081 --> 00:18:51.421
University, at first at least.

00:18:51.480 --> 00:18:54.021
Then, there was the authority figure.

00:18:54.381 --> 00:18:56.811
There was the scientific context.

00:18:57.091 --> 00:18:59.841
There was the graduated escalation that

00:18:59.841 --> 00:19:02.781
made each individual step seem small.

00:19:03.251 --> 00:19:05.401
Just 15 more volts, like

00:19:05.621 --> 00:19:07.671
a frog in boiling water.

00:19:09.041 --> 00:19:10.751
And there was the physical

00:19:10.931 --> 00:19:12.791
and psychological distance

00:19:12.981 --> 00:19:14.751
from the person being harmed.

00:19:15.291 --> 00:19:17.221
You were just pressing a button, you

00:19:17.221 --> 00:19:19.131
were not being forced to pick up an

00:19:19.141 --> 00:19:21.361
iron bar and beat someone with it.

00:19:22.461 --> 00:19:25.661
Together, all of this was enough to

00:19:25.761 --> 00:19:28.811
override what people knew, somewhere

00:19:28.931 --> 00:19:30.941
inside themselves, to be wrong.

00:19:31.681 --> 00:19:34.511
This is what Hannah Arendt believed

00:19:34.521 --> 00:19:36.651
she had seen in Eichmann, and

00:19:36.841 --> 00:19:38.621
it is what Milgram said he had

00:19:38.631 --> 00:19:40.561
demonstrated in a laboratory.

00:19:41.351 --> 00:19:43.801
People are deeply responsive to

00:19:43.891 --> 00:19:47.321
context, to authority, to the structures

00:19:47.591 --> 00:19:49.791
they find themselves inside, far

00:19:49.791 --> 00:19:52.031
more so than the simple categories

00:19:52.031 --> 00:19:54.501
of good and evil would suggest.

00:19:55.401 --> 00:19:58.441
Now, Milgram was at pains to clarify

00:19:58.641 --> 00:20:00.521
what his findings did not mean.

00:20:00.841 --> 00:20:02.541
He was not saying that all

00:20:02.541 --> 00:20:04.921
humans are potential Nazis.

00:20:05.051 --> 00:20:06.921
He was not saying that individual

00:20:06.921 --> 00:20:08.861
character is irrelevant.

00:20:09.401 --> 00:20:11.441
Some people did refuse.

00:20:11.821 --> 00:20:13.771
Some walked out of the experiment

00:20:14.031 --> 00:20:15.371
before reaching the maximum.

00:20:16.431 --> 00:20:19.061
What the results suggested, he said,

00:20:19.351 --> 00:20:22.161
was that obedience is far more common

00:20:22.351 --> 00:20:25.541
than we assume, and the conditions which

00:20:25.551 --> 00:20:29.201
produce it are neither rare nor exotic.

00:20:30.561 --> 00:20:33.011
The implication Milgram drew was

00:20:33.011 --> 00:20:35.901
a call for structural awareness.

00:20:36.321 --> 00:20:38.321
We know what the conditions are.

00:20:38.561 --> 00:20:39.581
The authority figure.

00:20:39.621 --> 00:20:41.551
The institutional context.

00:20:41.781 --> 00:20:43.511
The isolation from peers

00:20:43.761 --> 00:20:45.101
who might model dissent.

00:20:45.691 --> 00:20:49.231
If we know all that, then we can begin to

00:20:49.231 --> 00:20:51.651
build institutions that counteract them.

00:20:52.041 --> 00:20:54.721
We can train people to recognise

00:20:54.741 --> 00:20:56.461
that shift when they enter it.

00:20:56.821 --> 00:20:58.631
We can make refusal easier

00:20:58.951 --> 00:21:00.131
by making it visible.

00:21:01.521 --> 00:21:03.661
Obviously, this is easier said than

00:21:03.661 --> 00:21:07.011
done, but this experiment did valuable

00:21:07.011 --> 00:21:09.471
work in highlighting the conditions in

00:21:09.471 --> 00:21:11.571
which people can find themselves doing

00:21:11.591 --> 00:21:13.721
things they would not normally do.

00:21:14.761 --> 00:21:16.811
Now, of course, the experiment

00:21:16.821 --> 00:21:18.701
has had its fair share of critics.

00:21:19.201 --> 00:21:21.471
In the immediate aftermath, it was

00:21:21.471 --> 00:21:23.961
criticised on an ethical level.

00:21:24.571 --> 00:21:26.291
After all, the teachers

00:21:26.521 --> 00:21:28.131
had been deeply distressed.

00:21:28.991 --> 00:21:32.761
But the more important criticism was about

00:21:32.771 --> 00:21:35.511
what the findings actually meant, and

00:21:35.631 --> 00:21:37.991
whether the experiment was valid at all.

00:21:39.141 --> 00:21:41.621
The most serious objection is that

00:21:41.621 --> 00:21:43.551
participants may not have fully

00:21:43.551 --> 00:21:45.341
believed the shocks were real.

00:21:46.041 --> 00:21:49.381
Some of them, at some level, may

00:21:49.391 --> 00:21:51.121
have suspected they were in a

00:21:51.151 --> 00:21:53.511
controlled situation where harm

00:21:53.731 --> 00:21:55.301
would not actually be allowed.

00:21:56.291 --> 00:21:58.851
If that is true, the compliance

00:21:58.851 --> 00:22:00.521
figures are less alarming.

00:22:01.051 --> 00:22:02.791
They mean only that ordinary people

00:22:02.961 --> 00:22:05.601
are willing to play along in what they

00:22:05.601 --> 00:22:08.041
suspect is a performance, not that

00:22:08.041 --> 00:22:09.811
they are willing to harm a stranger.

00:22:11.210 --> 00:22:13.631
Now, to be fair to Milgram, he

00:22:13.631 --> 00:22:16.840
addressed this head-on, and he tried to

00:22:16.860 --> 00:22:19.091
figure out whether this was the case.

00:22:19.781 --> 00:22:22.201
He surveyed the participants afterwards,

00:22:22.421 --> 00:22:25.691
and the majority reported that they

00:22:25.701 --> 00:22:27.591
had believed the shocks were real.

00:22:28.231 --> 00:22:29.501
Some told him that the

00:22:29.501 --> 00:22:31.651
experience still haunted them.

00:22:32.791 --> 00:22:35.201
And other researchers have since

00:22:35.251 --> 00:22:37.521
examined the original transcripts

00:22:37.761 --> 00:22:40.951
and identified clear signs of real

00:22:40.981 --> 00:22:43.371
belief among most of the participants.

00:22:44.321 --> 00:22:46.351
In other words, they did believe

00:22:46.541 --> 00:22:48.081
they were inflicting real

00:22:48.081 --> 00:22:50.561
pain, but they did it anyway.

00:22:51.971 --> 00:22:54.441
Now, although the debate continues,

00:22:54.721 --> 00:22:57.191
Milgram's experiment is one of the most

00:22:57.201 --> 00:23:00.021
cited in the history of social psychology.

00:23:00.591 --> 00:23:02.771
It has been replicated many times

00:23:02.781 --> 00:23:06.031
since, in modified and more ethically

00:23:06.091 --> 00:23:09.531
cautious forms, and the core finding

00:23:09.771 --> 00:23:11.941
has proved remarkably robust.

00:23:12.631 --> 00:23:16.161
In 2009, a psychologist named Jerry

00:23:16.171 --> 00:23:19.041
Burger conducted a partial replication

00:23:19.281 --> 00:23:21.861
at Santa Clara University, stopping

00:23:21.861 --> 00:23:25.241
the experiment at 150 volts rather than

00:23:25.441 --> 00:23:27.331
allowing it to continue to the maximum.

00:23:28.371 --> 00:23:30.751
He found that around seventy per

00:23:30.751 --> 00:23:33.081
cent of participants were still

00:23:33.091 --> 00:23:35.041
willing to continue past that

00:23:35.041 --> 00:23:37.701
point, which is a figure consistent

00:23:37.851 --> 00:23:39.631
with Milgram's original results.

00:23:40.401 --> 00:23:43.191
Now, the uncomfortable thing for anyone

00:23:43.191 --> 00:23:45.021
listening to this is that you're probably

00:23:45.021 --> 00:23:47.941
thinking some variant of "I would

00:23:47.941 --> 00:23:50.591
never do that", but what the experiment

00:23:50.591 --> 00:23:53.341
suggests is that, "well, you're probably

00:23:53.351 --> 00:23:55.451
more likely to do it than you think".

00:23:56.801 --> 00:23:59.021
Milgram had a term for this.

00:23:59.211 --> 00:24:01.341
He called it the "agentic state."

00:24:02.081 --> 00:24:04.031
It is the psychological shift

00:24:04.341 --> 00:24:06.731
from seeing yourself as a moral

00:24:06.771 --> 00:24:08.961
agent, someone who makes their own

00:24:08.961 --> 00:24:11.521
decisions, to seeing yourself as an

00:24:11.621 --> 00:24:13.991
instrument of someone else's will.

00:24:14.930 --> 00:24:17.141
It's what soldiers describe when

00:24:17.141 --> 00:24:19.101
they talk about following orders.

00:24:19.540 --> 00:24:21.851
It's what corporate employees describe

00:24:22.071 --> 00:24:24.031
when they implement policies they

00:24:24.031 --> 00:24:26.211
find troubling but did not choose

00:24:26.441 --> 00:24:28.341
and cannot individually change.

00:24:29.121 --> 00:24:31.441
In a micro way, it's what we all

00:24:31.441 --> 00:24:33.401
experience when we are asked to do

00:24:33.401 --> 00:24:35.791
something we would not do on our own

00:24:35.801 --> 00:24:38.800
initiative, but we go through with because

00:24:38.801 --> 00:24:41.321
the organisation we belong to requires it.

00:24:42.231 --> 00:24:44.651
And Milgram is not judging

00:24:44.651 --> 00:24:46.691
us for it, or providing an

00:24:46.691 --> 00:24:48.544
answer for what to do about it.

00:24:48.991 --> 00:24:51.881
What he is saying, and this experiment

00:24:51.881 --> 00:24:53.861
suggests, is that the division

00:24:54.111 --> 00:24:57.061
between good and evil is not as

00:24:57.061 --> 00:24:59.521
clear-cut as we might like to think.

00:25:00.991 --> 00:25:03.121
OK, then, that is it for today's

00:25:03.121 --> 00:25:04.851
episode, the first part of

00:25:04.851 --> 00:25:06.801
our three-part mini-series on

00:25:06.801 --> 00:25:08.381
The Dark Side of Psychology.

00:25:08.741 --> 00:25:10.561
Next up we'll be moving into the

00:25:10.561 --> 00:25:13.221
disturbing world of Harry Harlow's monkey

00:25:13.221 --> 00:25:15.301
experiments, where the question of what

00:25:15.331 --> 00:25:17.731
love actually is was put to the test

00:25:17.751 --> 00:25:20.261
in a primate laboratory in Wisconsin.

00:25:20.651 --> 00:25:22.831
And in part three, we'll be heading to

00:25:22.831 --> 00:25:26.091
the psychiatric hospitals of 1970s America

00:25:26.341 --> 00:25:28.761
for the Rosenhan experiment, perhaps

00:25:28.811 --> 00:25:30.751
the most controversial study of all.

00:25:32.001 --> 00:25:33.781
You've been listening to English Learning

00:25:33.781 --> 00:25:36.581
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:25:36.951 --> 00:25:40.021
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:25:40.071 --> 00:25:42.741
I'll catch you in the next episode.

